## End-to-End: Managed Iceberg Table — Databricks → Snowflake on Azure

---Here are the complete end-to-end steps:

---

## Step 1 — Create Managed Iceberg Table in Databricks

```sql
-- No LOCATION clause — UC assigns the ADLS path automatically
CREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders (
    order_id    BIGINT,
    customer_id BIGINT,
    order_date  TIMESTAMP,
    amount      DOUBLE,
    status      STRING,
    region      STRING
)
USING iceberg
PARTITIONED BY (months(order_date), region)
TBLPROPERTIES (
    'write.format.default'         = 'parquet',
    'write.target-file-size-bytes' = '134217728'
);
```

---

## Step 2 — Note the UC-Managed ADLS Path

```sql
DESCRIBE EXTENDED my_catalog.my_schema.orders;
```

Note the auto-assigned `Location` — something like:
```
abfss://uc-metastore@mystorageaccount.dfs.core.windows.net/unity-catalog/<uuid>/orders
```

You need the **storage account name** and **container name** for the Snowflake setup.

---

## Step 3 — Create Azure Service Principal for Snowflake Auth to Databricks

```bash
# SP that Snowflake uses to call the UC Iceberg REST API
az ad sp create-for-rbac --name snowflake-uc-reader
```

Save the output (`appId`, `password`, `tenant`). Then grant it read access in Databricks:

```sql
GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG  my_catalog                TO `<sp-app-id>`;
GRANT USE SCHEMA  ON SCHEMA   my_catalog.my_schema      TO `<sp-app-id>`;
GRANT SELECT      ON TABLE    my_catalog.my_schema.orders TO `<sp-app-id>`;
```

---

## Step 4 — Create Storage Integration in Snowflake

```sql
-- Run as ACCOUNTADMIN
CREATE OR REPLACE STORAGE INTEGRATION uc_azure_storage_integration
    TYPE                      = EXTERNAL_STAGE
    STORAGE_PROVIDER          = AZURE
    ENABLED                   = TRUE
    AZURE_TENANT_ID           = '<your-azure-tenant-id>'
    STORAGE_ALLOWED_LOCATIONS = (
        'azure://<uc-storage-account>.blob.core.windows.net/<uc-container>/'
    );
```

---

## Step 5 — Grant Consent + RBAC in Azure

```sql
-- Retrieve Snowflake's managed SP details
DESC INTEGRATION uc_azure_storage_integration;
```

From the output:

| Field | Action |
|---|---|
| `AZURE_CONSENT_URL` | Open in browser as Azure AD admin → click Accept |
| `AZURE_MULTI_TENANT_APP_NAME` | Use this as the assignee in the RBAC command below |

```bash
# Grant Snowflake's SP read access to the UC-managed ADLS container
az role assignment create \
  --assignee "<AZURE_MULTI_TENANT_APP_NAME>" \
  --role "Storage Blob Data Reader" \
  --scope /subscriptions/<sub-id>/resourceGroups/<rg>/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/<uc-storage-account>/blobServices/default/containers/<uc-container>
```

---

## Step 6 — Create External Volume in Snowflake

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE EXTERNAL VOLUME uc_managed_iceberg_volume
    STORAGE_LOCATIONS = (
        (
            NAME                    = 'uc-adls-location'
            STORAGE_PROVIDER        = 'AZURE'
            STORAGE_BASE_URL        = 'azure://<uc-storage-account>.blob.core.windows.net/<uc-container>/'
            STORAGE_AZURE_TENANT_ID = '<your-azure-tenant-id>'
        )
    );

-- Verify setup
DESC EXTERNAL VOLUME uc_managed_iceberg_volume;
```

---

## Step 7 — Create Catalog Integration in Snowflake

```sql
-- OAuth M2M (recommended for production)
CREATE OR REPLACE CATALOG INTEGRATION databricks_uc_catalog
    CATALOG_SOURCE    = ICEBERG_REST
    TABLE_FORMAT      = ICEBERG
    CATALOG_NAMESPACE = 'my_catalog.my_schema'
    REST_CONFIG = (
        CATALOG_URI = 'https://<databricks-workspace-url>/api/2.1/unity-catalog/iceberg'
        WAREHOUSE   = 'my_catalog'
    )
    REST_AUTHENTICATION = (
        TYPE                = OAUTH
        OAUTH_TOKEN_URI     = 'https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/oauth2/token'
        OAUTH_CLIENT_ID     = '<sp-app-id>'
        OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET = '<sp-client-secret>'
        OAUTH_SCOPE         = 'all-apis'
    )
    ENABLED = TRUE;
```

> For dev/test, replace the `REST_AUTHENTICATION` block with `TYPE = BEARER` and a Databricks PAT.

---

## Step 8 — Create Iceberg Table in Snowflake

```sql
-- Snowflake resolves the managed path via UC REST — no hardcoded ADLS path needed
CREATE OR REPLACE ICEBERG TABLE my_schema.orders
    CATALOG            = 'databricks_uc_catalog'
    EXTERNAL_VOLUME    = 'uc_managed_iceberg_volume'
    CATALOG_TABLE_NAME = 'orders';
```

---

## Step 9 — Query and Schedule Refresh

```sql
-- Query the table
SELECT region, COUNT(*) AS orders, SUM(amount) AS revenue
FROM my_schema.orders
GROUP BY region;

-- Manual refresh after Databricks writes
ALTER ICEBERG TABLE my_schema.orders REFRESH;

-- Automate with a Snowflake Task
CREATE OR REPLACE TASK refresh_orders_task
    WAREHOUSE = my_warehouse
    SCHEDULE  = '15 MINUTE'
AS
    ALTER ICEBERG TABLE my_schema.orders REFRESH;

ALTER TASK refresh_orders_task RESUME;
```

---

## Full Checklist

```
Databricks
  ✅ Managed Iceberg table created (no LOCATION)
  ✅ UC-managed ADLS path noted from DESCRIBE EXTENDED
  ✅ Azure SP created and granted SELECT on table

Azure
  ✅ AZURE_CONSENT_URL opened and approved by Azure AD admin
  ✅ Snowflake managed SP granted Storage Blob Data Reader on UC container

Snowflake
  ✅ Storage Integration created (AZURE type)
  ✅ External Volume pointing to UC-managed ADLS path
  ✅ Catalog Integration with UC REST endpoint + OAuth M2M
  ✅ Iceberg Table created using CATALOG_TABLE_NAME
  ✅ Refresh Task scheduled and resumed
```

> **Important:** Never drop and recreate the managed table in Databricks — the ADLS path will change and you'll need to redo the Snowflake setup. Use `TRUNCATE` or `DELETE` for data resets instead.
